<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="NAD salvage (plants)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5381" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; The dinucleotides NAD and NADP are ubiquitous electron carriers and coenzymes that participate in almost every metabolic pathway in the cell |CITS: [Moat87][16714307]|. The biosynthesis of NAD in plants is achieved via two major routes - a &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; biosynthesis (see |FRAME:PYRIDNUCSYN-PWY|) and a salvage pathway (this pathway). The salvage pathway shown here summarizes the various routes of degradation and salvage of pyridine compounds that have been reported from plants |CITS: [4306794]|. Depending on the plant species investigated and/or environmental conditions, this cycle may involve a varying number of steps |CITS: [Wagner86b][Zheng04][15837705][Ashihara05]|. The pyridine nucleotide cycling pathway ensures the homeostatic replenishment of NAD when the coenzyme has been involved in NAD consuming reactions. The pathway is fairly similar to the bacterial variant (|FRAME:PYRIDNUCSAL-PWY|) with the exception that some enzymes, e.g. NMN amidohydrolase, NAD synthase (NH3-dependent) have not been identified in plants, and that plants are capable of by-passing enzymes under certain conditions, hence conferring a broader adaptability |CITS: [16714307]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The anaplerotic enzyme of the pyridine nucleotide cycle is |FRAME: EC-2.4.2.19| (QPRtase) (|FRAME:PYRIDNUCSYN-PWY|) forming |FRAME: NICOTINATE_NUCLEOTIDE| (nicotinate mononucleotide, NaMN) which may be considered as the start compound of this cycle |CITS: [Wagner84]| |CITS: [11198422]|. From there two different routes can be taken to produce |FRAME: NIACINE| (vitamin B&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;), an important intermediate that gives rise to several secondary metabolites, e.g. trigonelline (|FRAME:PWY-5110|). An enzyme catalyzing a direct conversion of |FRAME: NICOTINATE_NUCLEOTIDE| to nicotinate, |FRAME: EC-3.2.2.M4|, has been partially purified from tobacco. However, the existence of that enzyme has not been proven. Another route that comprises several enzymatic steps, including the formation of the main intermediates nicotinate adenine dinucleotide (NaAD), NAD, nicotinamide mononucleotide (NMN) and nicotinamide, has been confirmed. The enzyme that catalyzes the final step of the formation of nicotinate from nicotinamide, |FRAME: EC-3.5.1.19|, has been identified in several plants |CITS: [Wagner86b][13790768][Ashihara05][15837705]|. The reverse conversion, from |FRAME: NIACINAMIDE| to |FRAME: NICOTINAMIDE_NUCLEOTIDE|, which is catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-2.4.2.12|, has not been detected in plants |CITS: [Hunt04][15837705]|. The final step, closing the cycle by converting |FRAME: NIACINE| to |FRAME: NICOTINATE_NUCLEOTIDE| (NaMN), is catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-6.3.4.21| |CITS: [Wagner86][16658750]|. This enzyme expresses a high affinity towards |FRAME: NIACINE|. Unlike the enzyme from animals, the plant enzyme does not accept nicotinamide |CITS: [Wagner86b]|. The pathway displays two additional routes that can be taken (1) from |FRAME: NICOTINAMIDE_NUCLEOTIDE| to |FRAME: NIACINAMIDE| and (2) from |FRAME: NIACINE| to |FRAME: NICOTINATE_NUCLEOTIDE|, that proceed via a nucleotide riboside intermediate. The nucleoside ribosides, as well as some of the associated enzyme activities, have been found in plants |CITS: [16714307][Hunt04][15837705][Polya75][6295388]|, but the enzymes remain to be further characterized.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="AU4FS-1" />
    <Author name="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE" />
    <Author name="hartmut" />
  </Authors>
